  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 429 AM EDT WED AUG 19 2026\n\nAs the frontal boundary associated with Thursday's convection \nlingers near the area on Friday, shower and thunderstorm chances \nwill persist, with the highest probabilities during the afternoon \nalong and south of the Hal Rogers Parkway/KY Highway 80 corridor. \nBeyond Friday, models and ensemble guidance remain in broad \nagreement on a continued upper-level pattern featuring a ridge \nover the western CONUS and troughing across the east, though \namplitude differences remain. Another shortwave embedded within \nthe longwave trough is projected to drop out of Northwest Canada \ninto the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley Saturday into Sunday. This \nwill drop another cold front across the Commonwealth, though \nconvective coverage and intensity is uncertain due to frontal\npassage timing. A cool Canadian high pressure system follows that\ncold front to kick off the last full week of August. However, the\nquiet weather won't last very long as yet another shortwave \nfeature drops into the trough mid-week, bringing another cold \nfront with a renewed opportunity of convection across eastern \nKentucky, perhaps as early as Tuesday but more likely in the days\nbeyond the end of the forecast period. Temperatures will run not \nfar from seasonal norms, mostly in the lower to middle 80s for \nafternoon highs while nighttime lows range in the lower to mid \n60s. Some of the cooler sheltered valleys could dip into the 50s \nearly next week. Dew points could still be a bit sticky on Friday \n- not far from the 70F mark - but should become much more \ncomfortable for the rest of the period.",
